Kinora @PACKAGE_VERSION@
==========
@BUILD_NOTICE@A film player for AmigaOS 3.2 on a PiStorm with a Raspberry Pi 4 or CM4
under Emu68, in the style of Media Player Classic: one window, the picture
above simple playback controls, everything else in the menus. The Pi's
VideoCore decodes the picture, the sound plays through AHI, and text
subtitles are drawn into the picture. Films play in the window or full screen.


INSTALLING
----------

Double-click Install. It asks where the drawer "Kinora" shall be made (the
default is SYS:Utilities) and whether the two shell tools go to C:, and says
which of the parts Kinora needs (below) it did not find. Kinora can be
installed first and those parts later; until then films do not open.

  Kinora, its icon, Fonts/      the drawer chosen
  demuxer.library               LIBS:
  audiocodec.library            LIBS:
  ffmpeg.codec                  LIBS:audiocodec/
  DMXInfo, ACInfo               C: (if wanted)

A library that is already there is replaced when this archive's is newer, or
another build of the same version; before an older one replaces a newer one
the script asks.

To remove Kinora, delete its drawer, the three libraries above and the two
tools. Its settings are in ENVARC:Kinora.prefs.


WHAT IT NEEDS
-------------

  * AmigaOS 3.2 on a PiStorm with a Raspberry Pi 4 or CM4 under Emu68.
    A Pi 3 plays H.264 only and is untested.
  * videocodec.library 0.6 with its codecs (videocore.codec for H.264,
    rpivid.codec for H.265), from the Emu68 driver stack:
    https://github.com/rondoval/emu68-driver-stack
  * Picasso96 with VideoCore.card.
  * AHI.

For full screen, give the graphics card 32 MB with this line in config.txt
on the Pi's boot partition, then restart the Pi (Ctrl-Amiga-Amiga, or off
and on):

  dtoverlay=videocore,mem=32

Full screen keeps up to four pictures in the card's memory and needs all four
to play smoothly. Emu68's default of 16 MB holds one at 1920 by 1080 in
24 bit, beside the Workbench.


WHAT IT PLAYS
-------------

  Containers   Matroska and WebM (.mkv, .webm); MP4 and QuickTime (.mp4,
               .m4v, .mov); MPEG transport streams (.ts, .m2ts)
  Video        H.264; H.265 (HEVC) on a Pi 4 or CM4
  Audio        AAC (LC, HE, HE v2, also as LATM in transport streams); AC-3;
               E-AC-3; DTS; Dolby TrueHD; MP3; MP2; Opus; Vorbis; FLAC; PCM
               (8, 16, 24 and 32 bit, 32-bit float)
  Subtitles    SubRip, ASS/SSA and MP4 timed text inside the film; a SubRip
               file (.srt) beside it or opened by hand

Not played: other video codecs (MPEG-2, MPEG-4 part 2, VC-1, VP9, AV1),
picture subtitles (Blu-ray PGS, DVD VobSub, DVB), and anything over the
network. Sound with more than two channels is mixed down to stereo unless the
AHI unit's mode is a 7.1 one. Files over 2 GiB play from file systems that
can seek that far; PFS3 cannot.


USING IT
--------

Start Kinora from its icon, or from a shell with films to play:

  Kinora film.mkv other.mp4

Drop films on the window or on the icon, or open them from the Project menu.

  Space, or a click on the picture     play, pause
  A double click on the picture        full screen and back
  Cursor left, right                   ten seconds back, on
  F                                    full screen and back; Esc leaves it
  A, S                                 the next audio track, subtitle track
  PgUp, PgDn                           the playlist's film before, the next
  J (with the right Amiga key)         jump to a time
  I                                    statistics in the picture's corner

In full screen the controls come up along the screen's foot when the pointer
moves, and the picture stands above them meanwhile. Moving in a film lands on
the key frame before the place asked for, which can be some seconds early.

Subtitles come from the film or from a .srt file of the same name beside it.
They are drawn with the font DejaVu Sans, which lies in the drawer Fonts
beside the program and has Latin, Greek and Cyrillic letters. For other
scripts choose a TrueType or OpenType font that has them (Subtitles menu,
Font...). Characters are placed one by one from the left, so scripts that
join their letters or run from the right do not come out right. Italic and
bold are honoured, and so is a cue's wish to stand at the top. Where the
picture leaves a black bar under it, a cue that fits goes there.

The Audio menu chooses the AHI unit the sound goes to. Kinora plays 7.1 where
the unit's mode in AHI's prefs is a 7.1 one, and stereo otherwise, at the
film's own level; when it mixes surround sound down to stereo, its loudest
moments are softened rather than clipped. For that, while a film is loaded
Kinora holds the unit's sound hardware, and other programs may not be heard
through it meanwhile. With "Share the unit with other programs" (Audio menu)
Kinora plays through ahi.device the usual way instead: others can be heard
too, and Kinora plays 6 dB quieter. It shares the unit that way as well when
another program already holds it.

The unit, sharing it, the volume, the font and the last drawer are
remembered.


WHEN PICTURES ARE DROPPED, OR A FILM DOES NOT PLAY
--------------------------------------------------

The key I shows what is being played and how: the streams, pictures a
second, pictures dropped, what waits in the buffers, the clock.

On AmiKit, Executive moves programs' priorities by how much they use the
processor, and in full screen that costs Kinora pictures: in a heavy 1080p
film it dropped several times as many. To keep Kinora's own priorities, add
this line to S:Executive.prefs, with tabs between its fields, and reboot:

  TASK		Kinora*		NOSCHEDULE

ExecutivePrefs makes the same entry: on its Tasks page a new one of type TASK,
named Kinora*, its action NOSCHEDULE and its priority left empty.

Full screen with less than 32 MB for the graphics card (WHAT IT NEEDS, above)
plays less smoothly than in the window.

The two shell tools tell what the libraries make of a film:

  DMXInfo film.mkv      what demuxer.library makes of the file: container,
                        streams, chapters
  ACInfo                the audio codecs audiocodec.library decodes
  ACInfo film.mkv       decodes the film's audio and says what came out


DRIVING IT FROM OTHER PROGRAMS
------------------------------

Kinora's ARexx port is KINORA. Its commands: PLAY, PAUSE, STOP, SEEK <ms>,
OPEN <file>, ADD <file>, NEXT, PREVIOUS, FULLSCREEN, SIZE <width> <height>,
FONT <file>, SUBTITLES <file>, STATUS, STATISTICS, TRACKS, AUDIO, SUBTITLE,
UNIT, VOLUME, ASPECT, DELAY, CHAPTERS, PLAYLIST and QUIT. Answers come in
RESULT.


